<p>I really wanted to like it, but I'm gonna downvote.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">And&nbsp;here's&nbsp;why.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Close&nbsp;crits</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>There are occasional issues with clinical tone throughout the article. For example:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-4157-2 instances are aggressive and sporadic, and will act with hostility towards anyone/anything in their surroundings upon arrival.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>could have been phrased much more clearly. And the footnote about the GoI sounds, frankly, as though it were written <em>by</em> the GoI. It could be presented as a quotation from some source document, or set up with additional support elsewhere as a memetic effect, but as written it comes off as kind of sloppy.</p>
<p>The video log at the end was pretty much exactly what I was expecting based on the lead-in. There was definitely horror and there was definitely comedy, but for me they never really found a balance point that stuck. Instead of being creeped out or amused, in the end I was just sad that this delusional anomalous artist and/or businessperson is out there serially hurting dogs (and possibly people) because they've convinced themselves that that's a good thing to do.</p>
</div>
</div>
